{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.MassTheory.MagnusCurvatureCommutator","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.MassTheory.MagnusCurvatureCommutator","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.BigOperators.Ring.Finset","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Module.LinearMap.Defs","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Algebra.Defs","lean:module:Mathlib.LinearAlgebra.Trace","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic"],"declarations":6,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.trace_commSum_indep_of_cyclic","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.trace_commSum_indep_of_cyclic","kind":"theorem","line":115,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/MagnusCurvatureCommutator.lean#L115-L142","doc":"**(★★) THE `ρ = I` CONTROL IS WEIGHT-BLIND.**  Any two cyclic linear functionals agree on the\ncurvature sum — both give zero.  So a control that checks `tr (Ω₂) = 0` at trivial density tests\n**cyclicity and nothing else**: it carries no information whatever distinguishing one weight\nfamily from another.\n\nWhy this is worth stating as a theorem rather than an observation.  The Magnus-Ω₂ sign probes\n(`{deuteron,triton}_magnus_omega2_sign_probe.py`,\n`he4_magnus_omega2_sign_sparse_probe.py`) each report a `ρ = I` control confirming\n`tr(Ω₂) = 0`, and their docstrings correctly read that as *\"any nonzero sign REQUIRES the\ndensity\"*.  The tempting further step — that a passing control is evidence the aggregation\nmeasure is the right one — is what this refutes: the control passes for the quantum Plancherel\nmeasure and for the Ocneanu/Wenzl family alike, and would pass for any cyclic functional\nwhatsoever, including ones nobody would propose.\n\nConsequence for `qou-za3q` decision 1: the weight family cannot be chosen on the strength of\nthe control.  It is fixed instead by the Markov-trace characterisation —\n`QOU.SchurWeyl.HeckeCoset.markovTrace_unique` together with\n`QOU.SchurWeyl.SeminormalRep.eq_wtrace_of_markov`, which identify the unique Markov trace at\n`z_D = q³/(q²+1)` with the Ocneanu/Wenzl weighted seminormal trace.\n\nNote the scope precisely: this says the control cannot DISCRIMINATE.  It does not say the\ncontrol is worthless — a control that FAILED would mean the germ or the trace was broken, which\nis exactly how the probes use it. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.trace_commSum_eq_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.trace_commSum_eq_zero","kind":"theorem","line":105,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/MagnusCurvatureCommutator.lean#L105-L113","doc":"**The curvature sum is annihilated by every cyclic functional.**  `ρ = 1` in the vanishing\ncorollary above: the identity commutes with everything, so `tr (Ω₂) = 0` for ANY cyclic linear\n`tr`, in every representation, with no hypothesis on `tr` beyond cyclicity. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.trace_mul_commSum_eq_zero_of_commute","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.trace_mul_commSum_eq_zero_of_commute","kind":"theorem","line":93,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/MagnusCurvatureCommutator.lean#L93-L103","doc":"**The vanishing corollary.** If `ρ` commutes with every generator, the curvature trace is\nzero in every representation. This is the sharp statement of what `(★)` identifies as the source\nof the whole decay object: it is entirely a failure-to-commute effect. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.trace_mul_commSum_eq_sum_comm","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.trace_mul_commSum_eq_sum_comm","kind":"theorem","line":81,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/MagnusCurvatureCommutator.lean#L81-L91","doc":"**(★) The curvature trace is a commutator-with-`ρ` sum.**\n`tr (ρ · Σ_a [σ_a, σ_{a+1}]) = Σ_a tr ([ρ, σ_a] · σ_{a+1})`, in every representation — no\nBurau, no R-matrix, no family restriction. Sorry-free; the only hypothesis is cyclicity of `tr`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.trace_mul_commutator_eq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.trace_mul_commutator_eq","kind":"theorem","line":71,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/MagnusCurvatureCommutator.lean#L71-L79","doc":"**One commutator, rewritten onto `[ρ, σ_a]`.** For a cyclic linear functional,\n`tr (ρ · [x, y]) = tr ([ρ, x] · y)`. This is the whole content of `(★)`; the sum is a\n`Finset.sum_congr` over it. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.commSum","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.commSum","kind":"def","line":65,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/MagnusCurvatureCommutator.lean#L65-L69","doc":"The nearest-neighbour commutator sum `Σ_a [σ_a, σ_{a+1}]` — the projector curvature `Ω₂^P`\nwithout its constant `4/[2]_q²` prefactor. Mirrors `MagnusGaugeCurvatureHookColumn.curvatureSum`;\nrestated here so this file stands alone on the cyclicity argument. ","signatureChunk":0}]}